WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.568 --> 00:00:02.735
-Our next guest tonight is
an Emmy Award-winning comedian,

00:00:02.735 --> 00:00:04.670
director, and writer
you know from his work on

00:00:04.670 --> 00:00:08.040
"Saturday Night Live" and as
one-third of The Lonely Island.

00:00:08.040 --> 00:00:10.109
He is the author of
the new children's book

00:00:10.109 --> 00:00:12.444
"Little Fox
and the Wild Imagination,"

00:00:12.444 --> 00:00:13.512
which is available today.

00:00:13.512 --> 00:00:14.847
Please welcome back to the show

00:00:14.847 --> 00:00:16.749
our very good friend
Jorma Taccone.

00:00:16.749 --> 00:00:18.217
How are you, Jorm?

00:00:18.217 --> 00:00:20.687
-Hi. I'm so glad
you mentioned the Emmy because,

00:00:20.687 --> 00:00:22.755
you know, it's right here!
-[ Laughs ] Oh, you have it!

00:00:22.755 --> 00:00:25.891
You put the Emmy
in the background.

00:00:25.891 --> 00:00:28.661
I think it'd be very nice
if people who are thinking about

00:00:28.661 --> 00:00:32.265
buying a children's book
you wrote know what song

00:00:32.265 --> 00:00:33.799
the Emmy is for.

00:00:33.799 --> 00:00:35.935
-Uh, it was called
"Dick in a Box."

00:00:35.935 --> 00:00:37.136
-[ Laughs ]

00:00:37.136 --> 00:00:40.507
So, Jorm --
-It is a heartwarming tale.

00:00:40.507 --> 00:00:42.475
It's a heartwarming tale.
-I want to start by

00:00:42.475 --> 00:00:44.310
congratulating you on the fact

00:00:44.310 --> 00:00:47.213
that you just became
a father again.

00:00:47.213 --> 00:00:52.384
-I did. I did. Mere weeks ago.

00:00:52.384 --> 00:00:54.988
-And your new baby girl,
correct?

00:00:54.988 --> 00:00:57.056
-Yeah, Zany Quinn.

00:00:57.056 --> 00:00:59.959
I wanted to name her
Quinn Elizabeth,

00:00:59.959 --> 00:01:04.163
but Mari said that I couldn't
name our child a joke name.

00:01:04.163 --> 00:01:06.232
-[ Laughs ]

00:01:06.232 --> 00:01:10.536
I want to ask about Wylie,
your 5-year-old.

00:01:10.536 --> 00:01:14.206
Is not only the inspiration for
this book, but he actually --

00:01:14.206 --> 00:01:17.943
There's actual dialogue of
Wylie's in the book, correct?

00:01:17.943 --> 00:01:20.646
-Yes. Sort of the impetus of
this book was --

00:01:20.646 --> 00:01:22.815
It's obviously
the wild imagination

00:01:22.815 --> 00:01:24.317
of my 5-year-old, Wylie,

00:01:24.317 --> 00:01:27.253
but there was a quote
when he was 3 years old.

00:01:27.253 --> 00:01:30.456
He would constantly say, "Now, I
will put you in a mail truck

00:01:30.456 --> 00:01:34.427
and throw you in the ocean, and
a shark will eat you all gone,"

00:01:34.427 --> 00:01:36.596
which was I think
his way of telling me

00:01:36.596 --> 00:01:38.230
that he loved me very much.

00:01:38.230 --> 00:01:40.499
-It is a very nice thing.

00:01:40.499 --> 00:01:41.768
And I will say

00:01:41.768 --> 00:01:44.904
you've got a fantastic
illustrator for this book.

00:01:44.904 --> 00:01:46.372
There is Mail Truck and Shark.

00:01:46.372 --> 00:01:48.674
And it must be so exciting
to see the art

00:01:48.674 --> 00:01:53.112
come back for words like that,
especially words of your child.

00:01:53.112 --> 00:01:55.481
-Well, you know this, because
I'm doing the comic-book thing.

00:01:55.481 --> 00:01:56.949
I mean, like,
when you write something

00:01:56.949 --> 00:02:00.552
and then have it visually come
to life, it's just incredible.

00:02:00.552 --> 00:02:02.288
And this guy, Dan Santat,

00:02:02.288 --> 00:02:03.823
the fact that he even agreed
to do this --

00:02:03.823 --> 00:02:05.391
like, he's a Caldecott winner.

00:02:05.391 --> 00:02:06.993
I couldn't believe that he was

00:02:06.993 --> 00:02:08.461
even willing to be
involved with me.

00:02:08.461 --> 00:02:11.663
I mean, you know, but "Dick in
a Box" I guess goes a long way

00:02:11.663 --> 00:02:12.698
to certain people.
-[ Laughs ]

00:02:12.698 --> 00:02:13.867
Something else
that goes a long way

00:02:13.867 --> 00:02:16.469
with a lot of people
is "MacGruber."

00:02:16.469 --> 00:02:18.704
You are one of the co-creators
of "MacGruber" as well,

00:02:18.704 --> 00:02:21.206
with Will Forte
and John Solomon.

00:02:21.206 --> 00:02:24.878
And you are very kind
because you are now working on

00:02:24.878 --> 00:02:28.147
an eight-episode series
for Peacock

00:02:28.147 --> 00:02:32.117
for fans of "MacGruber,"
of which there are legion.

00:02:32.117 --> 00:02:34.887
And I believe I am
the front of that fan line.

00:02:34.887 --> 00:02:35.988
We are very excited about this.

00:02:35.988 --> 00:02:38.625
Where are you in the process?

00:02:38.625 --> 00:02:42.495
-We've been writing Monday,
Wednesday, Friday every week.

00:02:42.495 --> 00:02:45.932
And quite often we'll start
about a half an hour late

00:02:45.932 --> 00:02:49.569
because Will's off
doing Sea Captain lines.

00:02:49.569 --> 00:02:51.004
-Yes.
-So I'm pretty glad

00:02:51.004 --> 00:02:52.771
that that's over.
-[ Laughs ]

00:02:52.771 --> 00:02:56.408
-He would just be in
the background, like, you know,

00:02:56.408 --> 00:02:59.912
this little --
"Arggh, weird transition."

00:02:59.912 --> 00:03:01.847
But you would actually
get to hear, like,

00:03:01.847 --> 00:03:03.682
the five different takes
that he would do.

00:03:03.682 --> 00:03:07.653
-Yes, I think we both know
from our lucky collaborations

00:03:07.653 --> 00:03:09.155
with Will
that he is a perfectionist.

00:03:09.155 --> 00:03:10.656
And every day I would text him
and say,

00:03:10.656 --> 00:03:11.790
"Look, if you don't have time,

00:03:11.790 --> 00:03:13.359
you don't have to do
Sea Captain lines."

00:03:13.359 --> 00:03:17.063
And, of course, he's also
very amenable to helping.

00:03:17.063 --> 00:03:20.332
But I forget that he, of course,
was taking huge chunks of time

00:03:20.332 --> 00:03:21.900
out of your day,
so I'm very sorry.

00:03:21.900 --> 00:03:24.303
-And I'm being kind when I say
it was a half an hour.

00:03:24.303 --> 00:03:25.805
It was usually more
like an hour.

00:03:25.805 --> 00:03:27.907
-[ Laughs ]

00:03:27.907 --> 00:03:29.775
Hey, I also owe you
thanks for this.

00:03:29.775 --> 00:03:33.846
A few years ago your wife Mari

00:03:33.846 --> 00:03:36.348
was directing a film
in Pittsburgh,

00:03:36.348 --> 00:03:38.584
and my family goes to Pittsburgh
once a year,

00:03:38.584 --> 00:03:41.053
and I told you it was for
my mother's birthday.

00:03:41.053 --> 00:03:44.223
And so you came to
my mother's birthday party.

00:03:44.223 --> 00:03:49.061 line:0%
And you made her an airbrushed
picture with her face on it,

00:03:49.061 --> 00:03:51.897 line:0%
which is just genuinely great.

00:03:51.897 --> 00:03:54.166 line:0%
Also, it's very rare when
the person holding the shirt

00:03:54.166 --> 00:03:55.601 line:0%
is making the same expression.

00:03:55.601 --> 00:03:58.437 line:0%
[ Laughs ] But there it is.

00:03:58.437 --> 00:04:00.840
And we tell everyone,
because you can't quite read it,

00:04:00.840 --> 00:04:03.343
what the tagline
that you put on the shirt was.

00:04:03.343 --> 00:04:08.147
-Oh, I believe the tagline was
"Real G's move in silence."

00:04:08.147 --> 00:04:09.649
-[ Laughs ]

00:04:09.649 --> 00:04:12.985
-Which, to me, said grandmas.
Real grandmas move in silence.

00:04:12.985 --> 00:04:16.823
-I would love for you to have
seen the full conversation

00:04:16.823 --> 00:04:18.958
with my mom when I tried to
explain to her

00:04:18.958 --> 00:04:21.327
what "Real G's move in silence"
meant.

00:04:21.327 --> 00:04:23.996
-[ Laughs ]

00:04:23.996 --> 00:04:26.299
-One of the fans
of "MacGruber" --

00:04:26.299 --> 00:04:28.534
and I was joking earlier --

00:04:28.534 --> 00:04:31.204
the people who have
watched "MacGruber"

00:04:31.204 --> 00:04:35.040
or are a fan of "MacGruber" is
one of our finest directors,

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:38.177
Christopher Nolan,
who directed "Batman,"

00:04:38.177 --> 00:04:39.746
"Inception," among other things.

00:04:39.746 --> 00:04:43.048
And he -- How did you find out
that Christopher Nolan was a fan

00:04:43.048 --> 00:04:46.085
and did you ever get a chance
to talk to him about it?

00:04:46.085 --> 00:04:50.122
-That was when Anne Hathaway
was on "Jimmy Fallon,"

00:04:50.122 --> 00:04:53.259
and I got a call at the time
from the producer.

00:04:53.259 --> 00:04:55.728
I believe it was Michael
Shoemaker, your producer,

00:04:55.728 --> 00:04:58.664
who called and told me
that I had to watch

00:04:58.664 --> 00:05:00.699
when Anne Hathaway was
on the show that night.

00:05:00.699 --> 00:05:03.602
I watched,
and she told a story that was

00:05:03.602 --> 00:05:07.173
when they were shooting
"Batman," she knew things

00:05:07.173 --> 00:05:09.375
were going really well
when Christopher Nolan

00:05:09.375 --> 00:05:13.478
started to quote "MacGruber,"
which was shocking to me

00:05:13.478 --> 00:05:15.648
that he was a fan at all.

00:05:15.648 --> 00:05:18.384
And then I went to
a directors dinner.

00:05:18.384 --> 00:05:20.152
The DGA hosts
these directors dinners,

00:05:20.152 --> 00:05:22.655
and me and my wife went
because she is a filmmaker,

00:05:22.655 --> 00:05:24.590
a much better filmmaker than me.

00:05:24.590 --> 00:05:26.793
And she was like,
"Oh, Christopher Nolan's here.

00:05:26.793 --> 00:05:28.227
You got to go talk to him."

00:05:28.227 --> 00:05:29.962
And so I ran up to him
and was like,

00:05:29.962 --> 00:05:32.598
"Chris, I'm a director.

00:05:32.598 --> 00:05:34.367
I did this movie 'MacGruber.'"

00:05:34.367 --> 00:05:35.935
He's, like, "Oh, hi,"
and I was like,

00:05:35.935 --> 00:05:38.637
"Yeah, we're thinking about
doing this sequel pretty soon,

00:05:38.637 --> 00:05:42.241
and what do you
think about me putting,

00:05:42.241 --> 00:05:43.543
when the director title
comes up,

00:05:43.543 --> 00:05:45.844
it just says 'Christopher Nolan'
with an asterisk?

00:05:45.844 --> 00:05:48.580
And then after the movie
another asterisk,

00:05:48.580 --> 00:05:49.715
like a footnote comes up

00:05:49.715 --> 00:05:51.250
and says who
really directed the movie."

00:05:51.250 --> 00:05:54.353
And he was, like, "Well, you
know, I'll have to ask my wife."

00:05:54.353 --> 00:05:55.654
[ Laughs ]

00:05:55.654 --> 00:05:57.690
So we're still hoping.

00:05:57.690 --> 00:06:00.192
I'm asking him if he wants to --

00:06:00.192 --> 00:06:03.029
-I'm so confident that question
was never asked

00:06:03.029 --> 00:06:05.397
to Christopher Nolan's wife.

00:06:05.397 --> 00:06:08.934
"Honey, sit down. There's
a huge opportunity for us."

00:06:08.934 --> 00:06:11.804
Hey, buddy,
congratulations again.

00:06:11.804 --> 00:06:14.206
The book is really wonderful.

00:06:14.206 --> 00:06:16.775
Not in jest at all.
Both my kids really loved it.

00:06:16.775 --> 00:06:18.511
I'm so excited for "MacGruber."

00:06:18.511 --> 00:06:21.947
And congratulations to you
and Mari on the new baby.

00:06:21.947 --> 00:06:25.217
-Oh, thanks so much, and
congratulations for being back.

00:06:25.217 --> 00:06:27.286
-It's nice to be back,
and we did make Forte

00:06:27.286 --> 00:06:28.788
do a line tonight,
so I'm very sorry.

00:06:28.788 --> 00:06:30.122
[ Laughs ]
-Ugh!

00:06:30.122 --> 00:06:32.257
-"Little Fox and the Wild
Imagination" available today.

00:06:32.257 --> 00:06:33.726
We will be right back.

